{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"integer integer","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","fileName":"Integer.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Integer","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Number Systems and Arithmetic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","Opposite Number","Number Line","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"integer integer","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","fileName":"Integer.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Integer","heading":"Integer","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An integer is a whole number, including zero, a positive natural number, or the [[Opposite Number]] of a positive natural number:\n\n$$\\mathbb{Z}=\\{\\ldots,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,\\ldots\\}.$$\n\nIntegers extend the natural numbers so subtraction always has an integer result. Their signs can be interpreted as opposite directions on a [[Number Line]] or as gains and losses. Addition moves in the direction indicated by each sign, while multiplication by a negative reverses direction. Two sign reversals make a positive product; one makes a negative product.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","Opposite Number","Number Line","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"integer integer","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Integer.md","fileName":"Integer.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Integer","h